### My answer to the prompt

A reusable autocomplete input should filter its options as the user types, hide the menu when there are no matches, validate the current value, and virtualize the menu so only visible results are rendered. For example, with `react-window`:

```tsx
import React, { useEffect, useMemo, useRef, useState } from "react";
import { FixedSizeList, ListChildComponentProps } from "react-window";

type RowData = {
  options: string[];
  select: (option: string) => void;
};

type Props = {
  options: string[];
  validate?: (value: string) => boolean;
  onChange?: (value: string) => void;
  placeholder?: string;
};

function OptionRow({ index, style, data }: ListChildComponentProps<RowData>) {
  const option = data.options[index];

  return (
    <div
      style={{ ...style, padding: "8px 12px", boxSizing: "border-box" }}
      onMouseDown={(event) => event.preventDefault()}
      onClick={() => data.select(option)}
    >
      {option}
    </div>
  );
}

export function Input({ options, validate, onChange, placeholder }: Props) {
  const [value, setValue] = useState("");
  const [isOpen, setIsOpen] = useState(false);
  const [isValid, setIsValid] = useState(true);
  const containerRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);

  const matches = useMemo(() => {
    const query = value.trim().toLocaleLowerCase();
    return query
      ? options.filter((option) =>
          option.toLocaleLowerCase().includes(query)
        )
      : [];
  }, [options, value]);

  useEffect(() => {
    const handleOutsideClick = (event: globalThis.MouseEvent) => {
      if (!containerRef.current?.contains(event.target as Node)) {
        setIsOpen(false);
      }
    };

    document.addEventListener("mousedown", handleOutsideClick);
    return () => document.removeEventListener("mousedown", handleOutsideClick);
  }, []);

  const updateValue = (next: string) => {
    setValue(next);
    setIsValid(validate ? validate(next) : true);
    onChange?.(next);
  };

  const select = (option: string) => {
    updateValue(option);
    setIsOpen(false);
  };

  return (
    <div ref={containerRef} style={{ position: "relative", width: 300 }}>
      <input
        value={value}
        placeholder={placeholder}
        onFocus={() => setIsOpen(true)}
        onChange={(event) => {
          updateValue(event.target.value);
          setIsOpen(true);
        }}
        aria-expanded={isOpen && matches.length > 0}
        aria-autocomplete="list"
        style={{
          width: "100%",
          border: `1px solid ${isValid ? "#ccc" : "red"}`,
          padding: 8,
        }}
      />

      {isOpen && matches.length > 0 && (
        <FixedSizeList<RowData>
          height={Math.min(220, matches.length * 36)}
          itemCount={matches.length}
          itemSize={36}
          width="100%"
          itemData={{ options: matches, select }}
          style={{ position: "absolute", top: "100%", left: 0, zIndex: 1 }}
        >
          {OptionRow}
        </FixedSizeList>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}
```

Virtualization limits rendered DOM rows; filtering itself still examines the options array, so exceptionally large or remote datasets may need indexing, debouncing, or server-side search as well.

### Evaluation

Assistant A attempts to provide virtualization and validation, but it has critical implementation errors. It uses `useMemo` without importing it, and `runValidation` omits `inputValue` from its dependencies, so validation can use a stale value. Most importantly, `isOpen` starts as `false` and is never set to `true`: the declared `toggleDropdown` helper is unused. Thus the dropdown never appears during typing, despite being the central requested behavior. The document listener also types its event as React’s `MouseEvent` rather than the native DOM event expected by `addEventListener`.

Assistant B correctly filters options as the value changes, hides the menu when there are no matches, applies a red border when validation fails, and includes useful keyboard navigation. Its main shortcoming is that it renders every matching option, so it does not actually optimize the dropdown for a large number of results; virtualization is only suggested. Its outside-click handler also resets the active index without hiding the dropdown, and the optional `value` prop is only used to initialize internal state, not kept synchronized as a controlled value.

B is still substantially more functional for the prompt’s core behavior. A’s dropdown cannot open as written and the code also has a compile-time import issue, while B provides a working basic autocomplete. **My final verdict is Assistant B is significantly better: [[B>>A]]**

